# Semgrep path exclusions for the bedrock module scan: each path is left out of the scan,
# for the reason given above it.
#
# bedrock renders Terraform, YAML and Markdown from text/template: html/template's contextual
# escaping would corrupt that output, so the two files that execute those templates are
# excluded from the text-template audit rather than marked inline: hcltext.go, which executes
# the templates of the application's stack and of the organization's foundation and writes
# each value into an OpenTofu file as HCL takes it, and rootgithub.go, which renders the
# workflow of a root organization without a cloud. The GitHub test fake answers JSON to the
# client under test, so the direct-write audit (an HTML page writing user data) does not
# apply to it.
internal/render/hcltext.go
internal/org/rootgithub.go
internal/github/githubtest/server.go
# The migration renumber runs git with the repository's own paths (the default branch's
# tree, the tracked files, git mv), which is its purpose; the dangerous-exec audit does
# not apply to it.
internal/migration/git.go
# upgrade runs go install for a commit pin (the Go checksum database verifies the module),
# then runs the bedrock it installed, or the release it fetched and verified against the
# release's checksums file, with the render's own paths: running a program whose path it
# computed is the command's purpose; the dangerous-exec audit does not apply to it.
internal/cli/upgrade.go
# The test servers standing in for GitHub in the release package's and the upgrade
# command's tests answer JSON and a script to the client under test, as the GitHub test
# fake does; the direct-write audit (an HTML page writing user data) does not apply.
internal/release/release_test.go
internal/cli/upgrade_test.go
# The deploy steps' runner runs the programs a step drives (tofu, docker, an
# application's hook script through bash) with arguments the step builds: that is its
# purpose, so it is excluded from the dangerous-exec audit. git names a blob by the SHA-1
# of its content, and the migration guard computes that name to compare a file with the
# committed one: naming, not cryptography, so the weak-hash audit does not apply.
internal/deploy/exec.go
internal/migration/sequence.go
# The welcome image's program answers every request with its one page, a file embedded at
# build time that holds nothing a request sent, so the direct-write audit (an HTML page
# writing user data) does not apply. The page's one link is its icon, written into the
# link itself as a data address: nothing is fetched, so there is nothing for an integrity
# attribute to check. Its test starts the test binary itself as the program, with the
# arguments a Cloud Run service would pass it; the dangerous-exec audit does not apply.
welcome/main.go
welcome/page.html
welcome/main_test.go
